description:
Build `ChainedMap<K, V, Hash>`, a hash map that handles collisions by chaining:

```cpp
template <class K, class V, class Hash = std::hash<K>>
class ChainedMap
{
public:
    ChainedMap();                              // 8 buckets
    bool put(const K& key, const V& value);    // true if the key was new, false if it updated an existing entry
    V* find(const K& key);                     // nullptr if absent
    bool contains(const K& key) const;
    bool erase(const K& key);                  // true if something was removed
    V& operator[](const K& key);               // inserts V{} if absent, like std::unordered_map
    std::size_t size() const;
    std::size_t bucketCount() const;
    double loadFactor() const;
    std::size_t longestChain() const;
    template <class F> void forEach(F f) const;   // calls f(key, value) for every entry
};
```

- Each bucket is a chain (a `std::vector<std::pair<K, V>>` or a `std::list`); the bucket for a key is `Hash{}(key) % bucketCount()`.
- **Rehash** whenever an insertion makes `size() > bucketCount()`: double the bucket count and move every entry to its new bucket.
- The tests also use a deliberately terrible hash that sends every key to bucket 0, so your map must still be **correct** when everything collides.

